{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1202"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge literature/modern-lit","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/literature__modern-lit.g4.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",791],["+"," Eco's second novel, Foucault's Pendulum (1988), was never filmed, but it borrowed its title from a physical instrument: a Foucault pendulum, the long swinging weight that demonstrates the rotation of the Earth, which hangs in the Paris Musée des Arts et Métiers, where the book opens and ends."],["=",274]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",807],["+"," Ecos zweiter Roman, Das Foucaultsche Pendel (1988), wurde nie verfilmt, verdankt seinen Titel aber einem physikalischen Instrument: Gemeint ist ein Foucaultsches Pendel, jenes lange schwingende Gewicht, das die Drehung der Erde sichtbar macht und im Pariser Musée des Arts et Métiers hängt, wo das Buch beginnt und endet."],["=",268]]}]},
{"seq":3,"at":"2026-10-21","kind":"corrected","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1202"},"reason":"a1202 body: Name of the Rose \"shot at Kloster Eberbach\" → \"shot in Rome and at Kloster Eberbach\"; Foucault's Pendulum museum \"where the book opens and ends\" → \"where the book opens and where its climax takes place\"; DE body now names the German title \"Alexis Sorbas\" as the book's title — https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Name_of_the_Rose_(film) , https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foucault%27s_Pendulum","by":"enrich batch 118","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-enrich/enrich-report/a0926-a1358.md","sources":[{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Name_of_the_Rose_(film"},{"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foucault%27s_Pendulum"}]},
